<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter II of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1178">1178</a>–<a href="/years/1213">1213</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Peter the Catholic · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter II was king of Aragon and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1196. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-montpellier">Marie of Montpellier</a> in 1204 brought Montpellier to his house, and he fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. Defending his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, he was killed by Simon de Montfort's army at Muret in 1213.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Peter II of Aragon" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_II_de_Arag%C3%B3n_entero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> in July 1178, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a> and Sancha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1205 he acknowledged the feudal supremacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>, swearing to defend the Catholic faith, which gave him his epithet, the Catholic. He was the first king of Aragon to be crowned by the pope. In the first decade of the thirteenth century he commissioned the Liber feudorum Ceritaniae, an illustrated codex cartulary for the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdagne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conflent">Conflent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>.</p><p>On 15 June 1204 Peter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-montpellier">Marie of Montpellier</a>, daughter and heiress of William VIII of Montpellier, as her third husband. She gave him a son, James, but Peter soon repudiated her. Marie was popularly venerated as a saint for her piety and marital suffering, though she was never canonised, and she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1213.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Peter took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212, which marked the turning point of Muslim domination in the Iberian peninsula. The Crown of Aragon was widespread in the area that is now south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, then under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> princes such as the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. Pope Innocent III had called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>, who rejected the authority and teachings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> under Simon de Montfort began in 1209. It killed about 20,000 men, women and children, Cathar and Catholic alike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Returning from Las Navas in autumn 1212, Peter found that Montfort had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and exiled Count Raymond VI, his brother-in-law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and reached Muret in September 1213. Raymond urged him to avoid battle and starve out Montfort's forces, but the suggestion was rejected. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a> began on 12 September 1213. The Aragonese forces were disorganised and gave way under Montfort's assault, and Peter, caught in the thick of the fighting, was thrown to the ground and killed. His army broke in panic.</p><p>The conflict ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Meaux-Paris</a> in 1229, which agreed the integration of the Occitan territory into the French crown. Peter's kingdom passed to his only son by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-montpellier">Marie of Montpellier</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James the Conqueror</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter II of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The heaviest blows came during the Albigensian Crusade, when Simon de Montfort defeated Raymond VI and <b>Peter II of Aragon</b> at the Battle of Muret in 1213.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>With papal backing he gathered <b>Peter II of Aragon</b>, Sancho VII of Navarre and crusaders from beyond the Pyrenees, and on 16 July 1212 destroyed the Almohad army at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Muret in 1213 Simon de Montfort defeated the allied army of Raymond of Toulouse and <b>Peter II of Aragon</b>, who died there.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Bernard IV joined Raymond VI of Toulouse against the crusaders and was present at the battle of Muret in 1213, where <b>Peter II of Aragon</b> was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-comminges">County of Comminges</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1204 the heiress Marie of Montpellier married <b>Peter II of Aragon</b>; their son James I was born there in 1208.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-montpellier">Lordship of Montpellier</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Muret, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.46,1.33&amp;year=1200" aria-label="Muret on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Peter II of Aragon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5082 3859h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5082 3859h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.46,1.33&amp;year=1200">What was here in 1200? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
